  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe above mentioned warm moist conveyor is still looking on track to \nstream up and interact with the incoming frontal system to produce a \nwidespread rainfall event mainly from Saturday evening into Sunday \nmorning. With continued signs of an unseasonably PWAT feed of up to \n1 inch into the precip conveyor, model QPF amounts continue to \nincrease and now it appears that there will be a moderate to \npushing heavy rainfall potential with an axis of a half inch to 1 \ninch of rainfall from southwest IA into northeast WI by mid Sunday \nmorning. This includes the far northwestern DVN CWA northwest of CID \nand DBQ. The QPF potential then decreases to 0.15-0.25 in the \neastern CWA in IL. Synoptic lift/shear along the strong front and \nmarginal instability of 50-150 J/kg of MUCAPEs will support a chance \nfor a isolated rumbles of thunder Saturday night. A little more \ndeveloped cell may be able to do at least small hail. With the \nongoing FROPA, high temps on Sunday to be tricky with a substantial \nrange from northwest to east-southeast acrs the area. For now will \nadvertise highs in the mid 50s east, cooling to the mid 40s in the \npost-frontal northwestern CWA. Blustery northwesterly wind will cold \nadvect Sunday night into Monday morning.\n\nThe already advertised early next week chill down to normal or even \nslightly below normal temps still look on track. A passing bout of \nboth sfc and upper ridging should make for a dry Monday into \nTuesday, before a more progressive pattern acrs the CONUS shuttle \nsome upper trof energy in both the southern and northern streams \ntoward the entire MS RVR Valley stretch toward Wed. Will have to \nwatch for phasing potential for a possible resultant system to then \neffect the local area or get close to it. The models are still \nhaving run-to-run seasonal transition issues handling this mid next \nweek scenario, and even further out it appears a \"wavy\" zonal flow \npattern to emerge for a potentially more active period into the \nweekend following the holiday.",
